Fast loading: the scheduler can stop waiting for real time (EeScheduler::setTurbo), and the runtime counts what
the game reads and when a video is playing, so the host can decide when to use it.

What it adds: `setTurbo(true)` makes processDueDeadlines and waitForEvent skip their host-deadline waits (the guest
still sees every event at its emulated cycle, in the same order; only the wall time between them shrinks).
`setTurbo(false)` puts every pending host deadline back at its emulated distance from now, so pacing resumes
without a stall for the time gained. Executor thread only (the host's vblank callback runs there).
`include/runtime/don_read_counter.h`: g_donReadBytes (bytes from readCdSectors and fioRead) and g_donMpegActive
(bumped by sceMpegGetPicture, sceMpegGetPictureRAW8, sceMpegDemuxPssRing and sceMpegIsEnd, so a playing video is
seen). Nothing changes unless the host calls setTurbo.
Verified: the port's fight_short.txt and tools/run/scripts/konoha_ougi.txt run to their normal end with the host's
fast loading on (docs/journal/070).

